  "query_preview": "..(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 425 AM EDT SUN SEP 27 2026\n\nThe start of this period will be a continuation of the pleasant \nweather occurring in the near term, thanks to the approaching \nupper ridge from the west and sfc high pressure ridge that \ngradually shifts east of area into midweek. This pattern will keep\nus dry for the first half of the week and introduce a warming \ntrend starting Monday, with highs increasing a bit each day. Highs\nwill get back into the 80s by Tuesday or Wednesday, initially in \nthe more southern locations. There is some model discrepancy in \nwhat temps will be like for the second half of the week due to an \napproaching trough from the west. Right now it appears most \nlikely that we will remain in the 80s each day and maybe even \nclose to 90 in some areas if the Euro is correct. Lows should also\nmoderate throughout the week, though the pattern favors \nridge/valley temperature splits through at least Tuesday night.\n\nThe biggest uncertainty in this period is our rain chances and\ntiming of the best chances during the second half of the week and\ninto the weekend. Model guidance and NBM accordingly over the \npast couple days keeps pushing back the onset of 15 percent or\ngreater PoPs from Wednesday to Thursday or Thursday and now\ntrending toward Friday. This uncertainty originates from model \nvariability in the evolution of the upper level pattern which will\ndrive rain chances. The consensus of guidance is for the ridge \naxis initially west of the area at the start of the period \nmigrate across the Commonwealth and then off to the east during\nthe second half of the week as a trough also translates from the \nwest coast to the central US. The trough looks to slow down or \neven stall as it gets into the Central US as a ridge centered \nnear or east of the southeast U.S. coast and extending into the \nsoutheast sets up. It looks like one or more subtle shortwaves \nwill peel off the heart of the trough in the central US and ride \nthe edge of that southeast ridge. The trend in the delay of PoPs \nreturning to eastern KY appears due to that strong southeast \nridge. The strength and positioning of that ridge will probably \nresult in the greatest chances of meaningful rain remaining well \noff to our north and west until Friday or the weekend. NBM and \nLREF ensemble guidance shows a 50-55% chance of >.1\" of rain on \nSaturday and a 60-75% chance of >.1\" on Sunday. These higher PoPs \nfor the weekend have been pretty consistent over the past couple \nnights so it's something worth paying attention to. Now it's worth\nmentioning that although those higher PoPs have been consistent, \nthe upper level pattern that will deliver the active weather has \nnot been as consistent in the models. This will make determining \nhow much rain and timing of that into the region very hard to \nascertain at this time.",
